// Interview problems: Ashby, Shercliff & Cebon, Materials 4th ed., worked examples. Fields: id, title, q (statement), figs, figDesc, sol (examiner-only reference), answers.
(window.EXAMPLES_BY=window.EXAMPLES_BY||{}).mat=[{"id":"4.1","title":"Base stress in a tall brick column","q":"A brick column is 50 m tall. The bricks have density ρ = 1800 kg/m³ (take g = 9.8 m/s²). What is the axial compressive stress at its base, in MPa? Does the shape or size of the cross-section matter?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Weight force at the base F = ρAhg for cross-section area A and height h (self-weight only).\n2. Stress σ = -F/A = -ρgh; A cancels, so the result is independent of cross-section shape and size (same as hydrostatic pressure at depth h).\n3. σ = -1800 × 9.8 × 50 = -8.8×10⁵ Pa = -0.88 MPa (compressive).","answers":["σ = -0.88 MPa (compressive)","Independent of cross-section shape/size (area cancels)"]},{"id":"4.2","title":"Strains at the base of a brick column","q":"A 50 m tall brick column is made of bricks with density 1800 kg/m³, Young\u0027s modulus E = 25 GPa and Poisson\u0027s ratio ν = 0.2 (g = 9.8 m/s²). Find the axial strain and the transverse strain of the bricks at the bottom of the column.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Base stress from self-weight: σ = -ρgh = -1800×9.8×50 = -8.8×10⁵ Pa (compressive).\n2. Hooke\u0027s law: axial strain ε = σ/E = -8.8×10⁵ / 25×10⁹ = -3.5×10⁻⁵ (35 microstrain, contraction).\n3. Transverse strain ε_t = -νε = +7.0×10⁻⁶ (7 microstrain, small lateral expansion).","answers":["Axial strain ≈ -3.5×10⁻⁵ (35 με, compressive)","Transverse strain ≈ +7×10⁻⁶ (7 με, expansion)"]},{"id":"4.3","title":"Stiffening of a laterally constrained cube","q":"A cube is compressed uniaxially in direction 1. It is free to expand laterally in direction 2 (no stress there), but a rigid slot prevents any strain in direction 3 (ε₃ = 0). Using Hooke\u0027s law with Poisson\u0027s ratio ν, find the factor by which the effective modulus σ₁/ε₁ exceeds Young\u0027s modulus E, and evaluate it for (a) foam, ν ≈ 0; (b) metal, ν ≈ 0.33; (c) rubber, ν ≈ 0.5.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Direction 3: ε₃ = (1/E)[σ₃ - ν(σ₁+σ₂)] = 0 with σ₂ = 0, so σ₃ = νσ₁.\n2. Direction 1: ε₁ = (1/E)[σ₁ - νσ₃] = σ₁(1-ν²)/E.\n3. Effective modulus σ₁/ε₁ = E/(1-ν²), so the factor is 1/(1-ν²).\n4. (a) ν = 0: 1.00. (b) ν = 0.33: 1/(1-0.109) = 1.12. (c) ν = 0.5: 1/0.75 = 1.33.","answers":["Factor = 1/(1-ν²)","Foam 1.0; metal ≈ 1.12; rubber ≈ 1.33"]},{"id":"4.4","title":"Elastic energy stored in a stretched steel rod","q":"A steel rod has length L₀ = 10 m and diameter 10 mm. The steel has E = 200 GPa, elastic limit σ_y = 500 MPa and density 7800 kg/m³. The rod is stretched until the stress just reaches the elastic limit. Calculate the force in the rod, its extension and the elastic energy stored per unit volume. Then compare the elastic energy per unit mass with the chemical energy of gasoline (heat of combustion 43,000 kJ/kg).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. A = πd²/4 = π(0.01)²/4 = 7.85×10⁻⁵ m².\n2. F = σ_yA = 500×10⁶ × 7.85×10⁻⁵ = 39.3 kN.\n3. ε = σ/E = 500e6/200e9 = 0.0025; extension = εL₀ = 25 mm.\n4. W = σ²/2E = (500e6)²/(2×200e9) = 625 kJ/m³.\n5. Per mass: 625e3/7800 = 80 J/kg. Gasoline 43×10⁶ J/kg, so ratio ≈ 5.4×10⁵ (gasoline more than 500,000 times larger).","answers":["F ≈ 39 kN","Extension = 25 mm (ε = 0.25%)","W = 625 kJ/m³ = 80 J/kg, about 5×10⁵ times less than gasoline"]},{"id":"4.5","title":"Modulus and density of glass-reinforced polypropylene","q":"A composite has a polypropylene matrix containing 10% (by volume) glass reinforcement. Estimate its Young\u0027s modulus and density (i) if the glass is long parallel fibres (properties parallel to the fibres), and (ii) if the glass is small particles (the lower-bound modulus). Compare the specific moduli E/ρ of the matrix, the glass and the two composites.\nData: polypropylene E = 1 GPa, ρ = 900 kg/m³; glass E = 70 GPa, ρ = 2200 kg/m³.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Density (rule of mixtures, both cases): ρ = fρ_r + (1-f)ρ_m = 0.1×2200 + 0.9×900 = 1030 kg/m³.\n2. (i) Upper (parallel/Voigt) bound: E_U = fE_r + (1-f)E_m = 0.1×70 + 0.9×1 = 7.9 GPa.\n3. (ii) Lower (series/Reuss) bound: 1/E_L = f/E_r + (1-f)/E_m = 0.1/70 + 0.9/1, so E_L ≈ 1.1 GPa.\n4. Specific moduli (GPa·m³/kg): matrix 1.1×10⁻³; glass 31.8×10⁻³; fibre composite 7.9/1030 = 7.7×10⁻³ (about 7 times the matrix); particle composite 1.1/1030 = 1.07×10⁻³ (marginally below the matrix).","answers":["ρ = 1030 kg/m³ for both","Fibre composite E ≈ 7.9 GPa, E/ρ ≈ 7.7×10⁻³ GPa·m³/kg","Particle composite E ≈ 1.1 GPa, E/ρ ≈ 1.07×10⁻³ GPa·m³/kg","Matrix 1.1×10⁻³ and glass 31.8×10⁻³ GPa·m³/kg"]},{"id":"4.6","title":"How rare are the rare-earth elements?","q":"The rare-earth elements are used in advanced batteries, high-field permanent magnets, displays and sensors, and there is concern about their availability. Using the chart of the abundance of the elements in the Earth\u0027s crust, form an opinion on whether they are really that rare: compare their crustal abundance (in ppm) with that of lithium, cobalt, copper, lead, silver and the platinum-group elements.","figs":["figures/ex-mat/ex4-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Chart of abundance of the elements in the Earth\u0027s crust (ppm, log scale 10^-4 to 10^6) versus atomic number 0-100, with each element labelled, the rare-earth group outlined and the platinum group outlined.","sol":"1. Read the rare-earth group on the abundance chart: roughly 1 to 100 ppm by mass.\n2. Compare: similar to lithium, cobalt, copper and lead (order 10 to 100 ppm); about 100 times more abundant than silver; about 10,000 times more than the platinum-group elements.\n3. Conclusion: not especially rare in the crust; the issue is concentration/extractability and supply, not scarcity.","answers":["Rare earths ≈ 1-100 ppm, comparable to Li, Co, Cu, Pb","≈ 100× more abundant than silver, ≈ 10⁴× more than platinum group"]},{"id":"4.7","title":"Effective nuclear charge of aluminium versus calcium","q":"The effective nuclear charge is Z_eff = Z - S, where Z is the atomic number and S is the atomic number of the noble gas in the row above the element in the periodic table (a measure of core screening). Aluminium has Z = 13 and calcium has Z = 20. Find Z_eff for aluminium, and decide whether you would expect aluminium to have a larger or smaller atomic radius than calcium, giving reasons.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Neon (the noble gas above Al) has Z = 10, so Al: Z_eff = 13 - 10 = 3.\n2. Argon (above Ca) has Z = 18, so Ca: Z_eff = 20 - 18 = 2.\n3. Al has the larger effective charge, pulling its valence electrons in more strongly, and it is in row 3 while Ca is in row 4 (one extra filled inner shell screening Ca\u0027s valence electrons).\n4. So Al is expected to be the smaller atom.","answers":["Z_eff(Al) = 3","Al smaller than Ca (larger Z_eff and fewer filled shells)"]},{"id":"4.8","title":"Bond type in boron sulphide","q":"Boron sulphide is used in high-tech glasses. Use the electronegativity chart (Pauling scale) to decide whether you would expect its bonding to be metallic, ionic or covalent. Take ionic bonding to dominate when the electronegativity difference between the two atoms exceeds 1.7.","figs":["figures/ex-mat/ex4-8.webp"],"figDesc":"Plot of Pauling electronegativity versus atomic number for the elements, grouped by periodic-table row, with each element labelled and a dashed line dividing metals from non-metals.","sol":"1. Read electronegativity from the chart: boron ≈ 2.0, sulphur ≈ 2.5.\n2. Difference ≈ 0.5, far below 1.7, so not ionic.\n3. Both elements lie on the non-metal side of the metal/non-metal line, so not metallic.\n4. Conclusion: covalent bonding.","answers":["Δχ ≈ 0.5 \u003c 1.7","Both non-metals: covalent bonding"]},{"id":"5.1","title":"Second moment of area of a rectangular beam","q":"A beam has a solid rectangular cross-section of height h and width b. The second moment of area for bending about the horizontal centroidal axis X-X is I = ∫ y² dA, where y is measured from that axis. Derive I_XX in terms of b and h.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Take a horizontal strip of height dy and width b at distance y from X-X; dA = b dy.\n2. I = ∫ y² b dy from y = -h/2 to +h/2.\n3. = b[y³/3] between limits = (b/3)(h³/8 + h³/8) = bh³/12.","answers":["I_XX = bh³/12"]},{"id":"5.2","title":"Second moments of area of a steel ruler","q":"A steel ruler has a rectangular section, width w = 25 mm and thickness t = 1 mm. Calculate the second moment of area I_XX for bending about the axis X-X (parallel to the width, i.e. bending the ruler flat-wise through its thickness) and I_YY for bending about the perpendicular axis Y-Y (parallel to the thickness, i.e. bending in the plane of the ruler\u0027s width). Compare the two.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. For a rectangle with dimension h perpendicular to the bending axis and dimension b parallel to it, I = bh³/12.\n2. X-X (flexible): I_XX = wt³/12 = 25×1³/12 = 2.1 mm⁴.\n3. Y-Y (stiff): I_YY = tw³/12 = 1×25³/12 = 1300 mm⁴.\n4. Ratio = (w/t)² = 625: I_YY is over 600 times I_XX, because material is much farther from the Y-Y axis.","answers":["I_XX ≈ 2.1 mm⁴","I_YY ≈ 1300 mm⁴, about 625 times larger"]},{"id":"5.3","title":"Second moment of area of an I-section","q":"An I-section beam has overall external dimensions B (width) × H (height). Two identical rectangular notches, each of width b/2 and height h (total notched width b), are cut away from either side of the web, symmetric about and centred on the horizontal centroidal axis X-X. Using the standard result I = (width)(height)³/12 for a rectangle about its own centroidal axis, find I_XX of the I-section.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Enclosing rectangle B×H about X-X: I = BH³/12.\n2. Subtract the two notches, each (b/2)×h and each centred on X-X: each has I = (b/2)h³/12.\n3. I_XX = BH³/12 - 2(b/2)h³/12 = (BH³ - bh³)/12.\n4. Same as a hollow rectangular section with a b×h void. Valid because all rectangles share the same centroidal axis (otherwise the parallel-axis theorem would be needed).","answers":["I_XX = (BH³ - bh³)/12"]},{"id":"5.4","title":"Cantilever deflection of a loaded steel ruler","q":"A stainless steel ruler (E = 200 GPa) is 300 mm long, 25 mm wide and 1 mm thick. It is clamped as a horizontal cantilever with 250 mm protruding from a table edge, and a 10 N weight is hung from the free end (neglect the ruler\u0027s self-weight). Calculate the vertical deflection of the free end when the ruler is clamped with its 25 mm dimension horizontal (bending through the 1 mm thickness). How much stiffer would it be if clamped with the 1 mm dimension horizontal instead (bending through the 25 mm dimension)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Cantilever end load: δ = FL³/(3EI).\n2. Flexible orientation: I = wt³/12 = 2.08 mm⁴ = 2.08×10⁻¹² m⁴.\n3. δ = 10×0.25³/(3×200e9×2.08e-12) = 0.156/1.25 ≈ 0.125 m = 125 mm (book ≈ 124 mm with I = 2.1).\n4. Stiff orientation: I = tw³/12 = 1302 mm⁴, a factor (25/1)² = 625 larger, so 625 times stiffer.\n5. δ_stiff = 125/625 ≈ 0.2 mm (the book prints 0.02 mm, a slip).","answers":["δ ≈ 124-125 mm for the flexible orientation","≈ 625 times stiffer (I ratio 1300/2.1) in the stiff orientation, δ ≈ 0.2 mm"]},{"id":"5.5","title":"Polystyrene ruler matching steel ruler stiffness","q":"A new ruler is to be made of polystyrene (E = 2 GPa) with the same length (300 mm) and width (25 mm) as a stainless steel ruler (E = 200 GPa, thickness 1 mm). The polystyrene thickness is chosen so that its bending stiffness equals that of the steel ruler. Find the required thickness. Compare the masses of the two rulers (densities: stainless steel 7800 kg/m³, polystyrene 1040 kg/m³).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Equal bending stiffness requires equal flexural rigidity EI.\n2. I_steel = 25×1³/12 = 2.08 mm⁴. I_PS = (200/2) × 2.08 = 208 mm⁴ = 25t³/12.\n3. t³ = 12×208/25 = 100 mm³, so t = 4.6 mm.\n4. Masses: steel = 7800×0.3×0.025×0.001 = 58.5 g (≈ 59 g); polystyrene = 1040×0.3×0.025×0.0046 = 36 g.\n5. Polystyrene ruler is only about 38-40% lighter despite a density ~7.5 times lower, because it must be 4.6 times thicker.","answers":["t ≈ 4.6 mm","m_steel ≈ 59 g, m_PS ≈ 36 g (about 40% lighter)"]},{"id":"5.6","title":"Polar second moment of a thick-walled tube","q":"A thick-walled circular tube has inner radius r_i and outer radius r_o. The polar second moment of area is J = ∫ 2πr³ dr over the section (r measured from the axis). Derive J for the tube in two ways: (a) by superposition using the result for a solid cylinder of radius R, and (b) by direct integration.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Solid cylinder of radius R: J = ∫₀^R 2πr³ dr = πR⁴/2.\n2. (a) Superposition: both circles share the same axis, so J_tube = J(outer solid) - J(inner solid) = (π/2)(r_o⁴ - r_i⁴).\n3. (b) Direct: J = ∫ from r_i to r_o of 2πr³ dr = 2π(r_o⁴ - r_i⁴)/4 = (π/2)(r_o⁴ - r_i⁴). Both agree.","answers":["J = (π/2)(r_o⁴ - r_i⁴)","Solid cylinder J = πR⁴/2"]},{"id":"5.7","title":"Twist of a brass rod under torque","q":"A brass rod with shear modulus G = 40 GPa, length 200 mm and a solid circular cross-section of diameter 10 mm is twisted by a torque of 10 N·m. What is the angle of twist?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Solid circle: J = πR⁴/2 with R = 5 mm: J = π(0.005)⁴/2 = 9.8×10⁻¹⁰ m⁴.\n2. θ = TL/(GJ) = 10×0.2/(40e9 × 9.8e-10) = 2/39.3 = 0.051 rad.\n3. = 2.9°.","answers":["θ ≈ 0.051 rad ≈ 2.9°"]},{"id":"5.8","title":"Replacing a solid brass rod by a tube of equal mass","q":"A solid brass rod of diameter 10 mm and length 200 mm (G = 40 GPa) is to be replaced by a hollow tube of outer diameter 25 mm, same length, using the same amount of brass (same cross-sectional area). (a) Find the inner radius and polar second moment of area of the tube. (b) Find the angle of twist of the tube under a torque of 10 N·m. (c) Find the ratio of the twist angles (rod/tube) and the ratio of polar second moments (tube/rod).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Equal area: π(r_o² - r_i²) = πR² with r_o = 12.5 mm, R = 5 mm: r_i = √(12.5² - 5²) = 11.5 mm (wall ≈ 1 mm).\n2. J_tube = (π/2)(r_o⁴ - r_i⁴); with r_i = 11.5 mm this is ≈ 10.9×10⁻⁹ m⁴ (unrounded r_i = 11.46 mm gives 11.3×10⁻⁹). J_rod = 0.98×10⁻⁹ m⁴.\n3. θ_tube = TL/(GJ) = 10×0.2/(40e9×10.9e-9) = 4.6×10⁻³ rad ≈ 0.26°.\n4. θ_rod/θ_tube = 2.9°/0.26° ≈ 11 = J_tube/J_rod ≈ 11, since θ ∝ 1/J at fixed T, L, G.","answers":["r_i ≈ 11.5 mm; J ≈ 10.9×10⁻⁹ m⁴","θ ≈ 4.6×10⁻³ rad ≈ 0.26°","Twist ratio ≈ 11 = J ratio ≈ 11 (tube about 11 times stiffer)"]},{"id":"6.1","title":"Plastic work absorbed by a stretched steel rod","q":"A steel rod with yield strength σ_y = 500 MPa is uniformly stretched to a nominal strain of 0.1 (10%). Assume elastic-perfectly-plastic behaviour (constant stress σ_y during plastic flow) and neglect the elastic strain. Approximately how much plastic work per unit volume is absorbed? Compare it with the elastic energy per unit volume stored at the yield point, given E = 200 GPa.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Plastic work per unit volume = area under the stress-strain curve ≈ σ_y ε_f = 500×10⁶ × 0.1 = 50 MJ/m³.\n2. Elastic energy at yield W = σ_y²/2E = (500e6)²/(2×200e9) = 0.625 MJ/m³ (625 kJ/m³).\n3. Ratio = 50/0.625 = 80.","answers":["W_pl ≈ 50 MJ/m³","≈ 80 times the elastic energy at yield (625 kJ/m³)"]},{"id":"6.2","title":"True strain and true stress in a tensile test","q":"Find the true strain for each of the following nominal strains in a tensile test on a metal: (a) 0.2% (the proof-stress offset), and (b) 20% (typical of the onset of failure at maximum load). What is the ratio of true stress to nominal stress at these two points? Assume volume is conserved during plastic deformation.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. True strain ε_t = ln(1 + ε_n).\n2. (a) ln(1.002) = 0.002. (b) ln(1.2) = 0.182.\n3. Constant volume: A·L = A₀L₀, so σ_t = F/A = σ_n(L/L₀) = σ_n(1+ε_n).\n4. Ratio σ_t/σ_n = 1+ε_n: (a) 1.002 ≈ 1; (b) 1.2.","answers":["(a) ε_t = 0.002; (b) ε_t = 0.182","σ_t/σ_n = 1 + ε_n: ≈ 1 at 0.2%, 1.2 at 20%"]},{"id":"6.3","title":"Identifying an unknown metal from hardness","q":"An unidentified metal from a competitor\u0027s product has a Rockwell A hardness of 50 and a density of approximately 8800 kg/m³. Use the hardness-scale conversion chart to estimate its Vickers hardness and yield strength. Then locate it on the strength-density chart and say which material family it is most likely to be.","figs":["figures/ex-mat/ex6-3a.webp","figures/ex-mat/ex6-3b.webp"],"figDesc":"Two charts: (1) a hardness-scale comparison chart with parallel vertical scales for Rockwell A, Rockwell C, Vickers, Brinell, Mohs and approximate yield strength (MPa), joined by horizontal reference lines; (2) the log-log strength-density property chart (strength in MPa vs density in kg/m³) with labelled material-family bubbles.","sol":"1. From the conversion chart, Rockwell A 50 corresponds to Vickers hardness ≈ 500 (kg/mm²) and yield strength ≈ 1600 MPa (consistent with Hv ≈ σ_y/3).\n2. Plot σ_y ≈ 1600 MPa at ρ ≈ 8800 kg/m³ on the strength-density chart: near the top of the nickel-alloy bubble.\n3. Likely a high-strength nickel alloy.","answers":["Hv ≈ 500; σ_y ≈ 1500-1600 MPa","Likely a nickel alloy"]},{"id":"6.4","title":"Particle strengthening in an aluminium alloy","q":"A polycrystalline aluminium alloy contains a dispersion of hard particles of diameter 10⁻⁸ m with an average centre-to-centre spacing of 6×10⁻⁸ m measured in the slip planes. The shear modulus of aluminium is G = 26 GPa and the Burgers vector is b = 0.286 nm. Estimate the contribution of the particles to the shear strength (Orowan bowing, Δτ = Gb/L with L the free spacing between particles) and to the tensile yield strength of the polycrystal (σ_y ≈ 3τ).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Free spacing between particle surfaces: L = 6×10⁻⁸ - 1×10⁻⁸ = 5×10⁻⁸ m.\n2. Δτ = Gb/L = 26e9 × 0.286e-9 / 5e-8 ≈ 149 MPa (book: 154 MPa, using G = 27 GPa).\n3. Polycrystal: Δσ_y ≈ 3Δτ ≈ 446 MPa (book: 462 MPa).","answers":["Δτ ≈ 150 MPa","Δσ_y ≈ 450 MPa (book 462 MPa with G = 27 GPa)"]},{"id":"6.5","title":"Dislocation density in annealed and cold-worked aluminium","q":"Two 1 cm cubes of aluminium are produced in the annealed (soft) and cold-worked (hard) conditions, with dislocation densities of 10⁵ mm⁻² and 10⁹ mm⁻² respectively (length of dislocation per unit volume, mm/mm³). Assume a square array of parallel dislocations. Estimate: (a) the total dislocation length in each cube; (b) the spacing between dislocations in the cold-worked sample; (c) the ratio of the dislocation-hardening contribution to strength in the cold-worked sample to that in the annealed sample (assume strengthening ∝ √ρ_d).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Volume = 1000 mm³. (a) Annealed: 10⁵×1000 = 10⁸ mm = 100 km. Cold-worked: 10⁹×1000 = 10¹² mm = 10⁶ km (a million km).\n2. (b) Square array: each dislocation occupies d², so ρ_d = 1/d², d = 1/√ρ_d = 1/√(10⁹ mm⁻²) = 3.2×10⁻⁵ mm = 32 nm.\n3. (c) Strength contribution ∝ √ρ_d, so ratio = √(10⁹/10⁵) = 100.","answers":["100 km (annealed), 10⁶ km (cold-worked)","d ≈ 32 nm","Strength ratio = 100"]},{"id":"7.1","title":"Bending stress in a loaded steel ruler","q":"A stainless steel ruler 300 mm long, 25 mm wide and 1 mm thick is mounted as a cantilever with the flat face horizontal (bending through the 1 mm thickness; I = wt³/12 = 2.1 mm⁴) with 250 mm protruding, and a 10 N weight is hung from its free end. Calculate the maximum bending stress. Compare it with the yield strength range 170-1000 MPa for stainless steel, and with the direct stress produced when the same 10 N force pulls the ruler axially.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Maximum moment at the support: M = FL = 10×0.25 = 2.5 N·m.\n2. σ = M y_m/I with y_m = 0.5 mm: σ = 2.5×0.0005/(2.1×10⁻¹²) ≈ 595 MPa.\n3. This lies within 170-1000 MPa, so the ruler needs a fairly strong stainless steel to avoid yielding.\n4. Axial pull: σ = F/(wt) = 10/(25×1) = 0.4 MPa, about 1500 times smaller than the bending stress.","answers":["σ_max ≈ 595 MPa","Axial stress = 0.4 MPa, far below the bending stress"]},{"id":"7.2","title":"Yield and collapse torque of a brass rod","q":"A solid circular brass rod of diameter 10 mm has a tensile yield strength of 300 MPa (shear yield strength k = σ_y/2). Calculate the torque at first yield (surface reaches k) and the torque at full plastic collapse (whole section at k). Use polar second moment J = πR⁴/2 = 0.98×10⁻⁹ m⁴.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Shear yield k = σ_y/2 = 150 MPa.\n2. First yield: elastic torsion τ_max = TR/J, so T = kJ/R = 150e6 × 0.98e-9 / 0.005 = 29.4 N·m.\n3. Full plasticity: uniform shear stress k over the section: T = ∫ k r (2πr dr) = 2πR³k/3 = 2π(0.005)³×150e6/3 = 39.3 N·m.\n4. Collapse/first-yield ratio = 4/3.","answers":["T_yield ≈ 29.4 N·m","T_collapse ≈ 39.3 N·m"]},{"id":"7.3","title":"Burst speed and stored energy of a CFRP flywheel","q":"A flywheel disk has radius 150 mm and thickness 50 mm and is made of CFRP with strength 700 MPa and density 1600 kg/m³. For a thin spinning disk (ν = 1/3) the maximum (central) stress is σ_max = 0.42ρω²R² and the kinetic energy is U = (π/4)ρtω²R⁴. Find the burst speed (rad/s and rpm), the maximum kinetic energy it can store, and compare this with the chemical energy of an equal mass of gasoline (calorific value 43,000 kJ/kg).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Burst when σ_max = 700 MPa: ω = √(σ/(0.42ρR²)) = √(700e6/(0.42×1600×0.15²)) = √(4.63e7) ≈ 6800 rad/s ≈ 65,000 rpm.\n2. U = (π/4)(1600)(0.05)(6800²)(0.15⁴) ≈ 1.5 MJ.\n3. Mass m = πR²tρ = π×0.15²×0.05×1600 = 5.65 kg (≈ 5.7 kg).\n4. Gasoline: 43,000 kJ/kg × 5.7 kg ≈ 245 MJ, about 160 times U.","answers":["ω ≈ 6800 rad/s ≈ 65,000 rpm","U ≈ 1.5 MJ","Gasoline of same mass ≈ 245 MJ, ≈ 160× more"]},{"id":"7.4","title":"Hertz contact between a steel ball and race","q":"A steel ball bearing of radius R = 5 mm is pressed with a force F = 10 N onto a flat steel race. Both have E = 210 GPa and yield strength 400 MPa. Use the elastic contact relations (ν = 1/3): contact radius a ≈ 0.7(FR/E)^(1/3), relative displacement u ≈ (F²/(E²R))^(1/3), and maximum shear stress τ_max ≈ F/(2πa²). Calculate the contact radius, the relative displacement and the maximum shear stress, and compare the maximum shear stress with the shear yield strength (σ_y/2). Also find the stress if the 10 N were spread uniformly over a circle of radius 5 mm.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. a = 0.7(FR/E)^(1/3) = 0.7(10×0.005/210e9)^(1/3) = 0.7×(2.38×10⁻¹³)^(1/3) = 4.3×10⁻⁵ m ≈ 0.043-0.044 mm.\n2. u = (F²/(E²R))^(1/3) = (100/(4.41e22×0.005))^(1/3) ≈ 7.7×10⁻⁷ m ≈ 0.77-0.78 µm.\n3. τ_max = F/(2πa²) = 10/(2π(4.3e-5)²) ≈ 8.5×10⁸ Pa ≈ 850 MPa. (The book prints ≈ 180 MPa, which does not follow from its own a; these formulas give a value above the 200 MPa shear yield.)\n4. Uniform stress: 10/(π×0.005²) = 0.13 MPa, thousands of times lower: contact concentrates stress enormously.","answers":["a ≈ 0.044 mm","u ≈ 0.78 µm","τ_max ≈ 850 MPa from the given formulas (book quotes ≈ 180 MPa), versus 200 MPa shear yield","Uniform-load stress ≈ 0.13 MPa"]},{"id":"7.5","title":"Window corner stress in a pressurised fuselage","q":"An aircraft fuselage skin is aluminium (yield 300 MPa), 8 mm thick, a circular cylinder of radius 2 m with internal overpressure Δp = 0.5 bar (5×10⁴ N/m²). A window is nominally square (160 mm × 160 mm) with corner radii of 5 mm. Calculate the nominal hoop stress in the wall (σ = ΔpR/t) and estimate the peak stress near the window corner using K_sc = 1 + α(c/r_sc)^(1/2) with α = 2 for tension, taking c as half the window width and r_sc the corner radius. To avoid fatigue the maximum allowable stress is one-third of yield. Is a 5 mm corner radius suitable?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Nominal hoop stress σ = ΔpR/t = 5×10⁴×2/0.008 = 12.5 MPa.\n2. K_sc = 1 + 2√(80/5) = 1 + 2×4 = 9.\n3. Peak stress ≈ 9×12.5 ≈ 112 MPa.\n4. Allowable = 300/3 = 100 MPa. Peak exceeds it, so a 5 mm radius is unsuitable (fatigue risk; de Havilland Comet); corners need larger radii.","answers":["σ_nominal = 12.5 MPa","K_sc ≈ 9, σ_peak ≈ 112 MPa \u003e 100 MPa allowable","5 mm radius not suitable"]},{"id":"7.6","title":"Power to roll a steel strip","q":"A rolling mill has a pair of rolls of radius R = 100 mm turning at ω = 0.5 rad/s. A steel strip 1 m wide is reduced in thickness from 25 mm to 18 mm. (a) Calculate the nominal plastic strain in the strip. (b) Estimate the minimum drive power (lower bound: plastic work only, no friction or redundant work) if the steel is (i) at room temperature with σ_y = 480 MPa, and (ii) at 600°C with σ_y = 150 MPa. Plastic work per unit volume = σ_y ε_pl, and the volume fed into the roll bite per unit width per radian of roll rotation is about R·t₀.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. ε_pl = Δt/t₀ = 7/25 = 0.28.\n2. Energy balance per unit width: volume fed per rotation Δθ = R t₀ Δθ, plastic work = σ_y ε_pl R t₀ Δθ = σ_y Δt R Δθ. Work by the two rolls = 2TΔθ, so power for two rolls P = 2Tω = Rωσ_yΔt per unit width.\n3. (i) P = 0.1×0.5×480e6×0.007 = 1.68×10⁵ W ≈ 170 kW (strip width 1 m).\n4. (ii) Power ∝ σ_y: (150/480)×168 ≈ 52 kW ≈ 50 kW.","answers":["ε_pl = 0.28","P ≈ 170 kW cold-rolling at 480 MPa","P ≈ 50 kW hot-rolling at 150 MPa"]},{"id":"8.1","title":"Rulers with scratches: yield or fast fracture?","q":"Two rulers, one of stainless steel and one of polystyrene, are each loaded as a horizontal cantilever of length 250 mm. Each has a sharp transverse scratch of depth c = 0.2 mm near the base on the tensile side. For this surface-crack geometry take the geometry factor Y = 1.1 in K1 = Y σ √(πc). Fracture toughness K1c: steel 80 MPa·m^1/2, polystyrene 1 MPa·m^1/2. Yield strengths: steel about 600 MPa, polystyrene about 50 MPa. Calculate the stress needed to cause fast fracture in each ruler. Will each cantilever fail by yielding or by fast fracture?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Fast fracture when K1 = K1c, so σ* = K1c/(Y√(πc)).\n2. √(πc) = √(π × 2×10⁻⁴) = 0.02507 m^1/2; Y√(πc) = 0.02758.\n3. Steel: σ* = 80/0.02758 = 2.9×10³ MPa = 2.9 GPa, far above σy = 600 MPa, so the ruler yields before it can fracture.\n4. Polystyrene: σ* = 1/0.02758 = 36 MPa, below σy = 50 MPa, so it fractures (fast fracture) before yielding.","answers":["Steel: σ_f ≈ 2.9 GPa \u003e\u003e σy, fails by yielding","Polystyrene: σ_f ≈ 36 MPa \u003c σy, fails by fast fracture"]},{"id":"8.2","title":"Can the test machine break the compact specimen?","q":"A compact tension specimen is machined from an aluminium alloy with width w = 50 mm, thickness b = 20 mm and a sharp pre-crack of length c = 10 mm. For this geometry (c \u003c\u003c w) the stress intensity factor is K1 = 1.64 · F/(b·w) · √(πc), where F is the applied load. The test machine can deliver a maximum load of 50 kN. The alloy has fracture toughness K1c = 25 MPa·m^1/2. Is the machine capacity sufficient to fracture the specimen? What load is needed?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Fracture when K1 = K1c, so F* = K1c · b · w /(1.64 √(πc)).\n2. b·w = 0.020 × 0.050 = 1.0×10⁻³ m²; √(πc) = √(π × 0.010) = 0.1772 m^1/2.\n3. F* = 25×10⁶ × 1.0×10⁻³ /(1.64 × 0.1772) = 25,000/0.2907 ≈ 86 kN.\n4. 86 kN \u003e 50 kN, so the machine is not strong enough. Options: larger machine or thinner specimen (smaller b reduces F*, subject to validity limits on K1c tests).","answers":["F* ≈ 86 kN","Machine capacity (50 kN) is insufficient"]},{"id":"8.3","title":"Plastic zone sizes at the scratches in two rulers","q":"The stainless steel and polystyrene cantilever rulers have transverse surface scratches of depth 0.2 mm (steel: K1c = 80 MPa·m^1/2, σy ≈ 600 MPa; polystyrene: K1c = 1 MPa·m^1/2, σy ≈ 50 MPa; geometry factor Y = 1.1). The steel ruler is about 1 mm thick. Estimate the plastic zone size at fracture for each ruler, using r_y = K1c²/(Y² π σy²). Compare each with the crack depth (and ruler thickness) and conclude which ruler yields and which fractures.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. r_y = K1c²/(Y²πσy²), Y² = 1.21.\n2. Steel: r_y = 80²/(1.21 × π × 600²) = 6400/(1.3685×10⁶) = 4.7×10⁻³ m = 4.7 mm. This is much greater than the 0.2 mm crack depth and exceeds the ruler thickness, so the ruler yields.\n3. Polystyrene: r_y = 1²/(1.21 × π × 50²) = 1/9503 = 1.05×10⁻⁴ m ≈ 0.1 mm, smaller than the 0.2 mm crack, so linear elastic fracture mechanics applies and the ruler fractures.","answers":["Steel r_y ≈ 4.7 mm \u003e\u003e 0.2 mm crack: yields","Polystyrene r_y ≈ 0.1 mm \u003c 0.2 mm crack: fractures"]},{"id":"8.4","title":"Ceramic bars of different volume at equal survival probability","q":"A ceramic cylinder of length 50 mm and radius 5 mm has survival probability P when loaded in uniform tension at 100 MPa. A second batch of the same ceramic is made as bars of square section 15 mm × 15 mm and length 80 mm. The Weibull modulus is m = 10. At what tensile stress do you expect the second batch to have the same survival probability P?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Weibull weakest-link statistics: Ps = exp[-(V/V0)(σ/σ0)^m]. Equal survival probability requires V1σ1^m = V2σ2^m, so σ2 = σ1 (V1/V2)^(1/m). P itself, V0 and σ0 cancel.\n2. V1 = 50 × π × 5² = 3927 mm³; V2 = 80 × 15 × 15 = 18,000 mm³.\n3. σ2 = 100 × (3927/18000)^0.1 = 100 × (0.2182)^0.1 = 100 × 0.859 = 85.9 MPa.","answers":["σ2 ≈ 86 MPa","Larger volume gives a lower strength for the same survival probability"]},{"id":"9.1","title":"Fatigue life after raising the stress amplitude","q":"The high-cycle fatigue life of a component obeys Basquin\u0027s law, Δσ · N_f^b = constant, with b = 0.1. Under a sinusoidal stress of amplitude 100 MPa (range 200 MPa) with zero mean, the fatigue life is 200,000 cycles. What will the fatigue life be if the amplitude is increased to 120 MPa (range 240 MPa)? Comment on the sensitivity.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Basquin: Δσ1 N1^b = Δσ2 N2^b, so N2 = N1 (Δσ1/Δσ2)^(1/b).\n2. 1/b = 10; Δσ1/Δσ2 = 200/240 = 0.8333.\n3. N2 = 200,000 × 0.8333^10 = 200,000 × 0.1615 ≈ 32,300 cycles.\n4. A 20% stress rise cuts life by ~84%: fatigue life is very sensitive to stress level.","answers":["N2 ≈ 32,300 cycles","~84% reduction in life for a 20% increase in stress amplitude"]},{"id":"9.2","title":"Fatigue life of a component under a tensile mean stress","q":"A component obeys Basquin\u0027s law, Δσ · N_f^b = constant, with b = 0.1; with zero mean stress and amplitude 100 MPa (range 200 MPa) its life is 200,000 cycles. It is made of a material with tensile strength σ_ts = 200 MPa. What is the fatigue life if the amplitude stays at 100 MPa but a tensile mean stress of 50 MPa is superimposed? Use Goodman\u0027s rule to account for the mean stress.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Goodman: Δσ_m = Δσ_0 (1 - σ_m/σ_ts), so the equivalent zero-mean range is Δσ_0 = Δσ_m/(1 - σ_m/σ_ts).\n2. Equivalent amplitude: 100/(1 - 50/200) = 133 MPa (range 266.7 MPa).\n3. Basquin: N2 = N1 (Δσ1/Δσ2)^(1/b) = 200,000 × (200/266.7)^10 = 200,000 × 0.75^10 = 200,000 × 0.0563 ≈ 11,300 cycles (book quotes 11,550 using rounded 266).","answers":["Equivalent zero-mean amplitude ≈ 133 MPa","N ≈ 11,000-11,600 cycles (≈ 11,300)"]},{"id":"9.3","title":"Remaining life after a block at nonzero mean stress","q":"A component has Basquin exponent b = 0.1 and a zero-mean life of 200,000 cycles at amplitude 100 MPa. With a tensile mean stress of 50 MPa and amplitude 100 MPa (tensile strength 200 MPa, Goodman correction) its life is about 11,300 cycles. It is loaded for N1 = 5000 cycles at mean stress 50 MPa and amplitude 100 MPa, and then cycled about zero mean at amplitude 100 MPa until it breaks. Use Miner\u0027s rule of cumulative damage to find the number of further cycles N2 to failure.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Miner: Σ N_i/N_f,i = 1.\n2. Block 1 damage = 5000/11,300 = 0.44 (book uses 11,300-11,550).\n3. Block 2: N2/200,000 = 1 - 0.44 = 0.56.\n4. N2 = 0.56 × 200,000 ≈ 112,000 cycles; about half the life is used at each level.","answers":["N2 ≈ 112,000 cycles (≈ 1.1×10⁵)"]},{"id":"9.4","title":"Cyclically loaded polystyrene ruler with a scratch","q":"A polystyrene ruler (width w = 25 mm, thickness t = 4.6 mm) is a cantilever of length L = 250 mm carrying an end force cycled from 0 to 10 N. It has a transverse crack at the tensile-side base. K1c = 1 MPa·m^1/2; take K = Y σ √(πc) with Y = 1.1, and assume the crack is shallow so the stress at its tip equals the extreme-fibre bending stress. (a) What crack depth causes fast fracture when the force reaches 10 N? (b) If the initial scratch depth is c_i = 0.1 mm, how many force cycles before the ruler breaks? Crack growth obeys the Paris law dc/dN = A(ΔK)^m with m = 4 and A = 5×10⁻⁶ (c in metres, ΔK in MPa·m^1/2).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Extreme-fibre stress: σ = 6FL/(w t²) = 6×10×0.25/(0.025 × 0.0046²) = 28.4 MPa; Δσ = σ_max since σ_min = 0.\n2. (a) c* = K1c²/(π Y² σ²) = 1/(π × 1.21 × 28.4²) = 3.3×10⁻⁴ m = 0.33 mm.\n3. (b) dc/dN = A (Y Δσ)^4 π² c²; integrate from c_i to c*: N_f = [1/(A Y⁴ Δσ⁴ π²)] (1/c_i - 1/c*).\n4. A Y⁴ Δσ⁴ π² = 5×10⁻⁶ × 1.464 × 6.505×10⁵ × 9.870 = 47.0.\n5. 1/c_i - 1/c* = 10,000 - 3030 = 6970 m⁻¹.\n6. N_f = 6970/47.0 ≈ 148 cycles.","answers":["(a) c* ≈ 0.33 mm","(b) N_f ≈ 150 cycles (≈ 148)"]},{"id":"10.1","title":"Consistency of edge-crack formulas for tension and bending","q":"For a single edge crack of length c in a wide plate under uniform remote tensile stress σ, with c much less than the plate width, the stress intensity factor is K1 = 1.1 σ √(πc). For an edge-cracked beam of rectangular section (width b, thickness t) carrying a bending moment M, with the crack on the tensile face and c small compared to t, the tabulated result is K1 = 6.6 M √(πc)/(b t²). Show that the beam result is consistent with the tension result, and explain the key assumption about the stress state that makes this so.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Surface bending stress σ_max = M y/I with y = t/2, I = b t³/12, so σ_max = 6M/(b t²).\n2. Substituting σ = σ_max into K1 = 1.1 σ√(πc): K1 = 1.1 × 6M/(b t²) × √(πc) = 6.6 M √(πc)/(b t²), identical to the tabulated beam result.\n3. Key assumption: the linear decrease of bending stress below the surface is ignored; the crack sees the peak surface stress as if it were uniform tension. Valid only for shallow cracks, c \u003c\u003c t.","answers":["6.6 = 1.1 × 6 using σ_max = 6M/(bt²)","Assumes stress is uniform at its surface value over the crack depth, valid for c \u003c\u003c t"]},{"id":"10.2","title":"Scratched polystyrene ruler under a 10 N end load","q":"A polystyrene ruler of width b = 25 mm and thickness t = 4.6 mm is loaded as a 250 mm long cantilever with an end load of 10 N. It has a 0.25 mm deep transverse scratch across its top (tensile) surface near the support. For an edge crack in a beam in bending, K1 = 6.6 M √(πc)/(b t²). The fracture toughness of polystyrene is K1c = 0.7 MPa·m^1/2. Can the ruler carry the 10 N load without fast fracture? What end load causes fracture?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Cantilever moment near the support: M = F L.\n2. Fast fracture at K1 = K1c: F* = K1c b t²/(6.6 L √(πc)).\n3. Numerator: 0.7×10⁶ × 0.025 × (4.6×10⁻³)² = 0.370.\n4. Denominator: 6.6 × 0.25 × √(π × 0.25×10⁻³) = 1.65 × 0.02803 = 0.04624.\n5. F* = 0.370/0.04624 ≈ 8.0 N \u003c 10 N, so the ruler snaps below the target load.","answers":["F* ≈ 8.0 N","No: it fractures before 10 N is reached"]},{"id":"10.3","title":"Largest wall thickness of a leak-before-break pressure vessel","q":"A cylindrical steel pressure vessel of diameter 5 m holds an internal pressure p = 4 MPa. The fracture toughness of the steel is 110 MPa·m^1/2. What is the greatest wall thickness that can be used while ensuring that the vessel will leak rather than fracture? Assume a semi-circular surface crack of length c (so K1 = σ√(πc)) oriented normal to the maximum (hoop) stress, σ = pR/t.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Leak-before-break: the crack must grow through the wall, c = t, without fast fracture: K1c = σ√(πt).\n2. Hoop stress σ = pR/t, R = 2.5 m.\n3. K1c = (pR/t)√(πt) = pR√π/√t, so t = π p² R²/K1c².\n4. t = π × 4² × 2.5²/110² = 314.2/12,100 = 0.026 m.","answers":["t_max ≈ 26 mm"]},{"id":"10.4","title":"Proof test pressure for 2000 safe vessel cycles","q":"The cylindrical steel pressure vessel (diameter 5 m, K1c = 110 MPa·m^1/2) is built with wall thickness t = 15 mm and pressurised cyclically between 0 and p = 4 MPa (hoop stress σ = pR/t). It is to be proof tested to guarantee 2000 safe pressure cycles, failure being defined as the crack growing to the wall thickness (leak). Crack growth obeys dc/dN = A(ΔK)^4 with A = 3×10⁻¹⁴ (c in metres, ΔK in MPa·m^1/2); take K = σ√(πc). Find the largest initial crack size that takes 2000 cycles to reach the wall thickness, and hence the proof-test pressure needed to guarantee such a crack is absent (the proof stress being the stress that would fracture the steel with this crack). Is the proof stress reasonable if steels with this toughness have yield strengths of 500-1000 MPa?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Stress range Δσ = pR/t = 4 × 2.5/0.015 = 667 MPa.\n2. dc/dN = A π² Δσ⁴ c². Integrate: N = [1/(A π² Δσ⁴)](1/c_i - 1/t).\n3. A π² Δσ⁴ = 3×10⁻¹⁴ × 9.870 × 667⁴ = 0.0585; for N = 2000: 1/c_i - 1/t = 117.\n4. 1/t = 66.7 m⁻¹, so 1/c_i = 183.7, c_i ≈ 5.4 mm.\n5. Proof stress: σ_proof = K1c/√(π c_i) = 110/√(π × 0.0054) ≈ 845 MPa.\n6. p_proof = σ t/R = 845 × 0.015/2.5 ≈ 5.1 MPa.\n7. 845 MPa lies within 500-1000 MPa: acceptable provided a sufficiently high strength variant is used; otherwise reduce the proof stress (larger c_i, fewer guaranteed cycles).","answers":["Δσ ≈ 667 MPa","c_i ≈ 5.4 mm","σ_proof ≈ 845 MPa, p_proof ≈ 5.1 MPa","Acceptable only for the stronger steels (yield ≳ 850 MPa)"]},{"id":"11.1","title":"Force to drag a loaded pallet across concrete","q":"Wood sliding on concrete has a coefficient of friction of 0.4. How much horizontal force is required to slide a wooden pallet carrying 300 kg of equipment across a horizontal concrete floor? (Use g = 9.81 m/s²; neglect the pallet mass.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Amontons\u0027 law: F_s = μ F_n.\n2. F_n = m g = 300 × 9.81 = 2943 N.\n3. F_s = 0.4 × 2943 = 1177 N.","answers":["F_s ≈ 1.18 kN"]},{"id":"11.2","title":"Braking distance of a vehicle with locked wheels","q":"A vehicle travels at 100 km/h (28 m/s) on dry asphalt. The driver brakes suddenly, locking all wheels, and the vehicle slides to a stop. The coefficient of friction of rubber on dry asphalt is 0.95; assume Amontons\u0027 law holds. Find the stopping distance, and say how it depends on vehicle mass.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Kinetic energy U = ½ m v² is dissipated by friction work F_s d, with F_s = μ m g.\n2. μ m g d = ½ m v², so d = v²/(2 μ g).\n3. d = 28²/(2 × 0.95 × 9.81) = 784/18.64 = 42 m.\n4. Mass cancels, so stopping distance is independent of weight (also of the number or width of tyres).","answers":["d ≈ 42 m","Independent of mass"]},{"id":"11.3","title":"Wear depth of an oscillating dry bearing after a year","q":"A dry sliding bearing oscillates with amplitude a = 2 mm (each cycle covers a total sliding distance 4a) at frequency f = 1 Hz under a normal load of 10 N. The nominal contact area is 100 mm². The Archard wear constant for the pair is k_a = 10⁻⁸ MPa⁻¹, with specific wear rate U = k_a P (P the bearing pressure). The bearing runs continuously for 1 year (3.15×10⁷ s). How far will the surfaces have worn down?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Wear depth Δx = (volume removed)/(nominal area) = U × distance slid = k_a (F_n/A_n) × distance.\n2. P = 10 N/100 mm² = 0.1 MPa; k_a P = 10⁻⁹ (dimensionless).\n3. Distance slid = 4 a f t = 4 × 0.002 × 1 × 3.15×10⁷ = 2.5×10⁵ m.\n4. Δx = 10⁻⁹ × 2.5×10⁵ = 2.5×10⁻⁴ m = 0.25 mm.","answers":["Δx ≈ 0.25 mm"]},{"id":"11.4","title":"Archard constant estimate for a new bio-polymer","q":"A newly developed bio-polymer has a hardness of 10 Vickers (kg/mm²). Data for polymers cluster around a dimensionless wear constant K ≈ 10⁻⁶, where K = k_a H, with H the hardness of the softer surface in MPa. Estimate approximately the Archard wear constant k_a (in MPa⁻¹) for a bearing made of this polymer.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Convert hardness: 10 Vickers = 10 × 9.81 ≈ 100 MPa.\n2. K = k_a H, so k_a = K/H = 10⁻⁶/100 MPa.\n3. k_a ≈ 10⁻⁸ MPa⁻¹.","answers":["k_a ≈ 10⁻⁸ MPa⁻¹"]},{"id":"11.5","title":"Overheating time of an acetal bearing","q":"An acetal bearing is designed for brief, intermittent use at a bearing pressure of 0.1 MPa and sliding velocity 0.2 m/s. The Pv limit of acetal is about 0.12 MPa·m/s. (a) Is the bearing safely below its Pv limit? (b) The acetal component is 2 mm thick and is accidentally run continuously with no cooling or other heat loss. How long will it take to heat up by an average of 100 °C, if the coefficient of friction is 0.2? Acetal density 1400 kg/m³, specific heat capacity 1420 J/kg·K.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Pv = 0.1 × 0.2 = 0.02 MPa·m/s, well below 0.12.\n2. Frictional power per unit area = μ P v = 0.2 × 0.1×10⁶ × 0.2 = 4000 W/m², so energy per area = 4000 t.\n3. Energy needed per unit area = ρ × thickness × C_p × ΔT = 1400 × 0.002 × 1420 × 100 = 4.0×10⁵ J/m².\n4. t = 4.0×10⁵/4000 ≈ 100 s.","answers":["Pv = 0.02 MPa·m/s, safely below the 0.12 limit","t ≈ 100 s"]},{"id":"11.6","title":"Initial power to friction-weld two steel tubes","q":"Two steel tubes of diameter 30 mm and wall thickness 2 mm are friction welded: one is held and the other rotated against it at 1200 rpm under an axial load of 10 kN. The initial coefficient of friction is estimated at 0.5. Find the initial torque and power required. (Treat the thin wall as acting at the mean radius R = 15 mm.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Thin wall (t \u003c\u003c R): all friction acts at radius R, so T = μ F R.\n2. T = 0.5 × 10,000 × 0.015 = 75 N·m.\n3. ω = 1200 × 2π/60 = 125.7 rad/s.\n4. Power q = T ω = 75 × 125.7 = 9.4 kW.","answers":["T = 75 N·m","q ≈ 9.4 kW"]},{"id":"11.7","title":"Friction-welding power under sticking friction","q":"After several seconds of rotation the weld region of the steel tubes (diameter 30 mm, wall thickness 2 mm, rotated at 1200 rpm) has heated until the true contact area equals the nominal area (sticking friction), and the interface shear stress equals the shear yield stress k = σy/2. The high-temperature yield stress is 80 MPa. Derive an expression for the torque and power in this regime (thin-walled tube, mean radius R = 15 mm, wall t) and evaluate them.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Shear stress over the whole annulus is k = σy/2 = 40 MPa.\n2. Contact area ≈ 2π R t; friction force = k · 2π R t acting at radius R, so T = 2π k t R².\n3. T = 2π × 40×10⁶ × 0.002 × 0.015² = 113 N·m.\n4. ω = 1200 × 2π/60 = 125.7 rad/s; q = T ω = 113 × 125.7 = 14.2 kW.","answers":["T = 2π k t R² ≈ 113 N·m","q = Tω ≈ 14.2 kW"]},{"id":"12.1","title":"Sea level rise from warming oceans","q":"The volume of water in the Earth\u0027s oceans is V = 1.37 × 10⁹ km³. The oceans cover an area A = 3.61 × 10⁸ km² (71% of the Earth\u0027s surface). Take the average temperature of the water to be 10 °C, where the volumetric thermal expansion coefficient of water is α_V = 8.8 × 10⁻⁵ K⁻¹. If the oceans warmed uniformly by 2 °C, by how much would sea level rise? Assume the ocean area stays constant.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Thermal expansion of the volume: ΔV = α_V·V·ΔT.\n2) V = 1.37×10⁹ km³ = 1.37×10¹⁸ m³ (1 km³ = 10⁹ m³). ΔV = 8.8×10⁻⁵ × 1.37×10¹⁸ × 2 = 2.4×10¹⁴ m³ (= 2.4×10⁵ km³; the book prints 10⁸ m³, a units slip).\n3) Area A = 3.61×10⁸ km² = 3.61×10¹⁴ m².\n4) Rise = ΔV/A = 2.4×10¹⁴ / 3.61×10¹⁴ ≈ 0.67 m (book: 0.66 m). Assumes fixed ocean area, uniform warming, no ice melt.","answers":["ΔV ≈ 2.4×10¹⁴ m³","Sea level rise ≈ 0.66–0.67 m"]},{"id":"12.2","title":"Temperature rise that buckles a clamped steel rail","q":"A steel rail of square cross-section 25 mm × 25 mm is rigidly clamped, preventing both movement and rotation, at two points a distance L = 2 m apart. The rail is stress-free at 20 °C. The thermal expansion coefficient of steel is α = 12 × 10⁻⁶ K⁻¹. At what temperature will thermal expansion cause the rail to buckle elastically? (Young\u0027s modulus is not needed numerically.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Clamped-clamped (fixed-fixed) Euler column: F_crit = 4π²EI/L².\n2) I = t⁴/12 = (0.025)⁴/12 = 3.26×10⁻⁸ m⁴; A = 6.25×10⁻⁴ m².\n3) Fully constrained thermal strain gives compressive stress σ = EαΔT, so F = EαΔT·A.\n4) Equate: EαΔT·A = 4π²EI/L², E cancels: ΔT = 4π²I/(αAL²) = 4π²×3.26×10⁻⁸ / (12×10⁻⁶ × 6.25×10⁻⁴ × 4) = 1.287×10⁻⁶ / 3.0×10⁻¹⁰ ≈ 43 K.\n5) Buckling temperature = 20 + 43 ≈ 63 °C.","answers":["ΔT ≈ 43 K","Buckles at about 63 °C"]},{"id":"12.3","title":"Thermal diffusivity of a polymer-like solid","q":"A material has thermal conductivity λ = 0.3 W/m·K, density ρ = 1200 kg/m³ and specific heat C_p = 1400 J/kg·K. What is its thermal diffusivity?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Thermal diffusivity a = λ/(ρC_p).\n2) ρC_p = 1200 × 1400 = 1.68×10⁶ J/m³·K.\n3) a = 0.3 / 1.68×10⁶ = 1.79×10⁻⁷ m²/s.","answers":["a ≈ 1.8×10⁻⁷ m²/s"]},{"id":"12.4","title":"Delay before underfloor-heated tiles feel warm","q":"A room with underfloor heating has 4 mm thick tiles separating the heating elements from the floor surface. For a tile: thermal conductivity λ = 0.8 W/m·K, specific heat C_p = 800 J/kg·K, density ρ = 2200 kg/m³. The floor is cold when the heating is switched on. Approximately how long will it take before the floor surface begins to feel warm?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Heat reaches the surface after a diffusion time t ≈ x²/a (order-of-magnitude estimate).\n2) Diffusivity a = λ/(ρC_p) = 0.8/(2200×800) = 4.5×10⁻⁷ m²/s.\n3) x = 4×10⁻³ m, x² = 1.6×10⁻⁵ m².\n4) t ≈ 1.6×10⁻⁵ / 4.5×10⁻⁷ ≈ 35-36 s.","answers":["a ≈ 4.5×10⁻⁷ m²/s","t ≈ 36 s"]},{"id":"12.5","title":"Metal to carry heat from a silicon chip","q":"A metal is required to conduct heat away from a high power-density silicon chip. It must conduct heat well but differ as little as possible from silicon in its thermal expansion coefficient, to avoid thermal stress when the temperature changes. Silicon has α ≈ 2.5-4 × 10⁻⁶ K⁻¹. Suggest a metal (or metal family) and justify the choice using typical property values.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Requirements: high λ and α close to silicon\u0027s (small thermal strain mismatch).\n2) Common high-conductivity metals (Cu, Al, Ag) have α ≈ 17-23×10⁻⁶ K⁻¹, 5-8 times too large; Invar matches expansion but conducts poorly.\n3) Tungsten and its alloys have α ≈ 4-5×10⁻⁶ K⁻¹, the closest of the metals, and λ ≈ 170 W/m·K, which is good.\n4) Choice: tungsten (W alloys).","answers":["Tungsten and its alloys","Reason: lowest-expansion metals with good conductivity; α closest to silicon"]},{"id":"12.6","title":"Estimating specific heat of an unknown building material","q":"A locally available building material has density ρ = 2300 kg/m³. Without access to any further information or equipment, estimate its specific heat capacity C_p, and state the reasoning behind your estimate.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) For almost all solids the volumetric heat capacity ρC_p is approximately constant, about 3×10⁶ J/m³·K (Dulong-Petit: 3k_B per atom, per atomic volume).\n2) C_p ≈ 3×10⁶ / 2300 ≈ 1300 J/kg·K.","answers":["ρC_p ≈ 3×10⁶ J/m³·K for solids","C_p ≈ 1300 J/kg·K"]},{"id":"12.7","title":"Metals combining high conductivity and high strength","q":"A high-performance engine has a pressurised heat exchanger that needs a metal with both high thermal conductivity and high strength. Which three metal systems offer the best combination of these two properties?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Look for the highest λ combined with good yield strength (high λσ_y).\n2) Silver (λ ≈ 420 W/m·K), copper alloys (≈ 100-390) and aluminium alloys (≈ 100-240) have the highest conductivities with useful alloyed strengths.\n3) Answer: silver, copper alloys, aluminium alloys (nickel alloys are strong but conduct much less).","answers":["Silver, copper alloys, aluminium alloys"]},{"id":"12.8","title":"Phonon mean free path in silicon","q":"A sample of silicon has Young\u0027s modulus E = 150 GPa, density ρ = 2300 kg/m³ and specific heat C_p = 680 J/kg·K. Its thermal conductivity is λ = 145 W/m·K. Treat heat conduction as carried by phonons moving at the speed of sound c_o = √(E/ρ), with λ = (1/3)ρC_p ℓ_m c_o. Find the mean free path ℓ_m of the phonons.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) c_o = √(E/ρ) = √(1.5×10¹¹/2300) = 8.08×10³ m/s (the book prints E as 1500 GPa, a misprint; 150 GPa reproduces its result).\n2) ℓ_m = 3λ/(ρC_p c_o).\n3) ρC_p = 2300 × 680 = 1.564×10⁶ J/m³·K.\n4) ℓ_m = 3×145 / (1.564×10⁶ × 8.08×10³) = 435 / 1.264×10¹⁰ = 3.4×10⁻⁸ m ≈ 0.034 µm (34 nm).","answers":["c_o ≈ 8.1×10³ m/s","ℓ_m ≈ 3.4×10⁻⁸ m (0.034 µm)"]},{"id":"12.9","title":"Thermal shock resistance of three oven-door glasses","q":"The temperature of a domestic oven door can reach 200 °C. If cold water is accidentally spilt on the hot door it suffers a thermal shock. Treat the surface as fully constrained by the interior so that the surface stress for a temperature difference ΔT is σ = EαΔT/(1 − ν). Find the thermal shock resistance ΔT_s (the largest sudden temperature change that does not cause fracture, with tensile strength σ_t as the limit) for three glass-based materials:\n- Soda-lime glass: σ_t = 30 MPa, E = 62 GPa, α = 9×10⁻⁶ K⁻¹, ν = 0.2\n- Borosilicate KG33: σ_t = 32 MPa, E = 63 GPa, α = 3.19×10⁻⁶ K⁻¹, ν = 0.19\n- Glass ceramic 9608: σ_t = 71 MPa, E = 86 GPa, α = 0.9×10⁻⁶ K⁻¹, ν = 0.25\nWhich would survive the oven door?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Fracture when σ = σ_t, so ΔT_s = σ_t(1 − ν)/(Eα).\n2) Soda-lime: 30×0.8/(62×10³×9×10⁻⁶) = 24/0.558 = 43 °C.\n3) Borosilicate: 32×0.81/(63×10³×3.19×10⁻⁶) = 25.9/0.201 = 129 °C.\n4) Glass ceramic: 71×0.75/(86×10³×0.9×10⁻⁶) = 53.25/0.0774 = 688 °C.\n5) A hot door at 200 °C meeting cold water gives a shock of roughly 180-200 °C: only the glass ceramic survives; soda-lime and borosilicate would crack.","answers":["Soda-lime: ΔT_s ≈ 43 °C","Borosilicate KG33: ≈ 129 °C","Glass ceramic 9608: ≈ 688 °C; only this exceeds the ~200 °C shock"]},{"id":"12.10","title":"Phase-change material to buffer room temperature","q":"In a climate with large day-night temperature swings, passive radiators filled with a phase-change material (PCM) that melts at about 20 °C are proposed: they absorb heat when ambient exceeds 20 °C and release it when it falls below. Two candidate PCMs are: C17 paraffin wax (melting point ≈ 20 °C, latent heat of melting 240 kJ/kg) and ice/water (melting point 0 °C, latent heat 334 kJ/kg). Which would you choose, and what mass of it is needed to provide 1 kW·h of energy management?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) The PCM\u0027s melting point must sit at the target temperature, 20 °C: paraffin C17 does, ice (0 °C) does not, so choose the paraffin despite its smaller latent heat.\n2) 1 kW·h = 3600 kJ.\n3) Mass = 3600 kJ / 240 kJ/kg = 15 kg.\n(The book notes ethylene glycol as an alternative needing ~21 kg.)","answers":["Choose C17 paraffin wax (melting point matches 20 °C)","15 kg for 1 kW·h"]},{"id":"13.1","title":"Tar cube squashed under a steel plate","q":"A tar has viscosity η = 10⁸ Pa·s. A cube of tar with a cross-sectional area A = 0.01 m² is placed under a steel plate of mass m = 0.5 kg (use g = 9.81 m/s²). The tar flows as an incompressible viscous solid. By how much will the cube shorten in 1 day?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Cube edge = √0.01 = 0.1 m. Stress σ = mg/A = 0.5×9.81/0.01 = 490 Pa.\n2) For uniaxial flow of an incompressible viscous solid, ε̇ = σ/(3η) = 490/(3×10⁸) = 1.6×10⁻⁶ s⁻¹.\n3) t = 1 day = 8.64×10⁴ s, so strain ε = ε̇t ≈ 0.14 (constant-stress, small-strain estimate).\n4) Shortening = 0.14 × 100 mm ≈ 14 mm.","answers":["ε̇ ≈ 1.6×10⁻⁶ s⁻¹","Strain ≈ 0.14, shortening ≈ 14 mm"]},{"id":"13.2","title":"Creep rate and extension of a hot stainless tie","q":"A stainless steel tie of length L = 100 mm is loaded in tension at stress σ = 150 MPa at temperature T = 800 °C. Its steady-state creep law is ε̇ = ε̇_o (σ/σ_o)ⁿ exp(−Q_c/RT) with ε̇_o = 10⁶ s⁻¹, σ_o = 100 MPa, n = 7.5, Q_c = 280 kJ/mol; R = 8.314 J/mol·K. What is the creep rate of the tie, and how much will it extend in 100 h (ignore elastic and primary strain)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) T = 1073 K. Q_c/RT = 280000/(8.314×1073) = 31.39, exp(−31.39) = 2.34×10⁻¹⁴.\n2) (σ/σ_o)ⁿ = 1.5^7.5 = 20.9.\n3) ε̇ = 10⁶ × 20.9 × 2.34×10⁻¹⁴ ≈ 4.8-4.9×10⁻⁷ s⁻¹ (book: 4.8×10⁻⁷).\n4) t = 100 h = 3.6×10⁵ s: ε = ε̇t ≈ 0.173.\n5) ΔL = 0.173 × 100 mm ≈ 17 mm.","answers":["ε̇ ≈ 4.8×10⁻⁷ s⁻¹","Strain ≈ 0.17, extension ≈ 17 mm"]},{"id":"13.3","title":"Sensitivity of creep to pressure and temperature","q":"A chemical plant component is made of the stainless steel with creep law ε̇ = ε̇_o (σ/σ_o)ⁿ exp(−Q_c/RT), n = 7.5, Q_c = 280 kJ/mol, R = 8.314 J/mol·K. Internal pressure generates a hoop stress of 150 MPa and the hoop creep rate is 4.8×10⁻⁷ s⁻¹ at T = 800 °C. (a) By what factor does the creep rate increase if the pressure is raised by 20%, and what is the new rate? (b) If instead the pressure is held constant but the temperature is raised from 800 °C to 850 °C, by what factor is the creep life t_f reduced? (Assume the Monkman-Grant law ε̇_ss·t_f = constant.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"(a) Hoop stress ∝ pressure, so σ rises to 180 MPa. Rate ratio = (180/150)^7.5 = 1.2^7.5 = 3.9; new rate ≈ 1.9×10⁻⁶ s⁻¹.\n(b) T₁ = 1073 K, T₂ = 1123 K. Rate ratio = exp[(Q_c/R)(1/T₁ − 1/T₂)] = exp[33680 × (9.320×10⁻⁴ − 8.905×10⁻⁴)] = exp(1.398) ≈ 4.0.\nMonkman-Grant: t_f = C/ε̇_ss, so life falls by the same factor, about 4.\nSmall changes in stress or temperature give large changes in rate and life.","answers":["(a) factor ≈ 3.9; ε̇ ≈ 1.9×10⁻⁶ s⁻¹","(b) creep life reduced by a factor ≈ 4.0"]},{"id":"13.4","title":"Oxygen ion diffusion in an alumina fuel-cell membrane","q":"Alumina (Al₂O₃) is suggested for the membrane of a fuel cell. Cell performance depends on the diffusion coefficient of oxygen ions in the membrane at the operating temperature, a maximum of T = 1100 °C. If D \u003c 10⁻¹² m²/s the cell is impractical. For oxygen diffusion in Al₂O₃: activation energy Q_d = 636 kJ/mol and pre-exponential D_o = 0.19 m²/s (R = 8.314 J/mol·K). Will the cell work?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) D = D_o exp(−Q_d/RT), T = 1373 K.\n2) Q_d/RT = 636000/(8.314×1373) = 55.7.\n3) exp(−55.7) = 6.2×10⁻²⁵, so D = 0.19 × 6.2×10⁻²⁵ ≈ 1.2×10⁻²⁵ m²/s.\n4) This is about 10¹³ times smaller than 10⁻¹² m²/s, so the cell is not practical.","answers":["D ≈ 1.2×10⁻²⁵ m²/s at 1100 °C","D ≪ 10⁻¹² m²/s: the cell will not work"]},{"id":"13.5","title":"Time to carburise a steel surface","q":"A low-carbon steel component (0.2 wt% C) is to be hardened by diffusing carbon into its surface from a carbon-rich environment at T = 1000 °C, where the maximum carbon solubility is C_s = 1.6 wt% C. At this temperature the iron is FCC austenite, with diffusion constant for carbon D_o = 8×10⁻⁶ m²/s and activation energy Q_d = 131 kJ/mol (R = 8.314 J/mol·K). The aim is a carbon concentration of at least 0.9 wt% to a depth of 0.2 mm. Estimate the time needed.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) T = 1273 K. D = D_o exp(−Q_d/RT) = 8×10⁻⁶ × exp(−131000/(8.314×1273)) = 8×10⁻⁶ × exp(−12.38) = 3.35×10⁻¹¹ m²/s.\n2) Diffusion depth x ≈ √(Dt), so t ≈ x²/D.\n3) x² = (2×10⁻⁴)² = 4×10⁻⁸ m².\n4) t ≈ 4×10⁻⁸ / 3.35×10⁻¹¹ ≈ 1.2×10³ s ≈ 20 min.","answers":["D ≈ 3.35×10⁻¹¹ m²/s","t ≈ 1200 s ≈ 20 minutes"]},{"id":"13.6","title":"Strain rate, stress and load for hot forging","q":"A Ni alloy billet is to be hot forged between flat parallel dies at 1100-1120 °C. The initial billet is a cylinder of diameter 20 cm and height 50 cm with its axis vertical. The top platen descends at an initial speed of 5 cm/s and comes to rest when the billet has been shortened by 2.5 cm. Assume constant deceleration (speed varies linearly with time). (a) Estimate the initial and average strain-rate and the duration of the deformation. (b) Reading the alloy\u0027s deformation-mechanism map gives a flow stress of about 70 MPa at this strain-rate and temperature, while the room-temperature yield stress is about 200 MPa. By what factor is the hot flow stress lower than the room-temperature yield stress? Which creep mechanism would you expect to dominate? (c) Estimate the forging load, assuming the axial stress needed is about 1.5 times the uniaxial flow stress (because of die friction).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"(a) Initial strain-rate = speed/height = 0.05/0.5 = 0.1 s⁻¹. With linearly decreasing speed the average speed is 2.5 cm/s, so average strain-rate ≈ 0.05 s⁻¹ and time = 2.5 cm / 2.5 cm/s = 1 s.\n(b) 200/70 ≈ 2.9, about 3 times lower. At such a high strain-rate the mechanism is power-law creep (diffusional flow is far too slow).\n(c) Axial stress = 1.5 × 70 = 105 MPa. Area = π×0.2²/4 = 0.0314 m². Force = 105×10⁶ × 0.0314 ≈ 3.3 MN.","answers":["ε̇ initial 0.1 s⁻¹, average ≈ 0.05 s⁻¹, duration ≈ 1 s","Hot flow stress ≈ 3 times lower than room-temperature yield; power-law creep","Load ≈ 3.3 MN"]},{"id":"14.1","title":"Oxide mass gain on a metal after 1000 hours","q":"A metal oxidises at 500 °C in a protective, parabolic manner, Δm² = k_p·t, where Δm is the mass gain per unit area and k_p = 3×10⁻¹⁰ kg²/m⁴·s. What is the mass gain per unit area after 1000 h?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Parabolic law: Δm = (k_p t)^½.\n2) t = 1000 h = 3.6×10⁶ s.\n3) k_p t = 3×10⁻¹⁰ × 3.6×10⁶ = 1.08×10⁻³ kg²/m⁴.\n4) Δm = 0.033 kg/m².","answers":["Δm ≈ 0.033 kg/m²"]},{"id":"14.2","title":"Oxide thickness of magnesium after 1000 hours","q":"A metal oxidises at 500 °C with parabolic rate constant k_p = 3×10⁻¹⁰ kg²/m⁴·s, so the oxygen mass gain per unit area after 1000 h is Δm = 0.033 kg/m². The metal is magnesium and the oxide is MgO (density 3550 kg/m³). Atomic weights: Mg 24.3 kg/kmol, O 16 kg/kmol. How thick will the oxide film be after 1000 h?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Δm is the mass of oxygen taken up. MgO has mass 40.3 per 16 of oxygen, so oxide mass per area = (40.3/16) × 0.033 = 0.083 kg/m².\n2) Thickness x = mass per area / density = 0.083/3550 = 2.3×10⁻⁵ m.\n3) x ≈ 23 µm.","answers":["Oxide mass ≈ 0.083 kg/m²","Thickness ≈ 23 µm"]},{"id":"14.3","title":"Effect of lower temperature on MgO film thickness","q":"After 1000 h at T₀ = 500 °C a MgO film is 23 µm thick. The film grows parabolically, with a growth constant that follows an Arrhenius law governed by the activation energy for oxygen diffusion in MgO, Q_d = 370 kJ/mol (R = 8.314×10⁻³ kJ/mol·K). How thick would the oxide be after 1000 h if the temperature were T₁ = 450 °C instead?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Parabolic growth: x ∝ (k_p t)^½ and k_p ∝ exp(−Q_d/RT), so x ∝ exp(−Q_d/2RT).\n2) x₁/x₀ = exp[−(Q_d/R)(1/T₁ − 1/T₀)]^½ with Q_d/R = 370/8.314×10⁻³ = 4.45×10⁴ K; T₁ = 723 K, T₀ = 773 K.\n3) 1/723 − 1/773 = 8.95×10⁻⁵; product = 3.98; half = 1.99; ratio = e^−1.99 = 0.137.\n4) x₁ = 0.137 × 23 µm ≈ 3.1-3.2 µm.","answers":["x₁/x₀ ≈ 0.137","x₁ ≈ 3.1 µm (about 3.2 µm with 23.3 µm)"]},{"id":"14.4","title":"Chloride concentration in sea water","q":"Sea water contains 35 g of salt (NaCl) per litre. What is the molar concentration of Cl⁻ ions in sea water? Atomic weights: Na 22.99 kg/kmol, Cl 35.45 kg/kmol.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Each NaCl dissociates into one Na⁺ and one Cl⁻, so [Cl⁻] = [NaCl].\n2) Molecular weight NaCl = 22.99 + 35.45 = 58.44 g/mol.\n3) Concentration = 35/58.44 = 0.599 mol/litre.","answers":["[Cl⁻] ≈ 0.60 mol/litre (0.6 M)"]},{"id":"14.5","title":"Reduction potential of zinc in dilute solution","q":"A zinc electrode is put in an electrolyte that contains 2 g of zinc (as Zn²⁺ ions) per litre of water. Its standard reduction potential is E° = −0.76 V. What is its corrected reduction potential at 25 °C? The atomic weight of zinc is 65.4 kg/kmol.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Nernst equation: E = E° + (0.059/z) log₁₀(C_ion), z = 2 for Zn²⁺.\n2) C_ion = 2/65.4 = 0.031 M.\n3) log₁₀(0.031) = −1.51, so correction = 0.0295 × (−1.51) = −0.045 V.\n4) E = −0.76 − 0.045 = −0.805 V.","answers":["C_ion ≈ 0.031 M","E ≈ −0.805 V"]},{"id":"14.6","title":"Charge needed to electroplate nickel","q":"What product of current density (A/m²) and time is needed to electroplate a 10 µm thick layer of nickel onto an object? Nickel density is 8910 kg/m³, atomic weight M = 58.7 kg/kmol (58.7 g/mol), valence z = 2, Faraday\u0027s constant F = 96,500 C/mol. Then state how long it takes at 1 A/m².","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Faraday: m = ItM/(zF), so per area I_A·t = m_A zF/M.\n2) Volume per area = 10⁻⁵ m³/m²; m_A = 8910 × 10⁻⁵ = 0.0891 kg/m² = 89.1 g/m².\n3) Moles/m² = 89.1/58.7 = 1.52 mol/m². I_A t = 1.52 × 2 × 96500 = 2.93×10⁵ C/m² (A·s/m²).\n4) At 1 A/m²: t ≈ 2.93×10⁵ s ≈ 81 h.\n(The book prints 293 A·s/m², a factor 1000 low from mixing kg and g.)","answers":["I_A·t ≈ 2.9×10⁵ A·s/m²","About 81 h at 1 A/m²"]},{"id":"14.7","title":"Material choices for pipework carrying 10% NaOH","q":"You must choose materials for pipework to handle 10% sodium hydroxide (caustic soda). Using general corrosion-resistance knowledge (e.g. the ranking tables in the textbook\u0027s appendix), identify materials you might use. Consider material combinations as well as single materials.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Materials rated excellent (A) in NaOH: stainless steels, copper alloys, titanium alloys, and the polymers polyethylene and polypropylene.\n2) Combinations: a plain steel pipe lined with PE or PP, giving strength from the steel and corrosion resistance from the lining.\n3) Materials commonly used to contain NaOH: stainless steels, nickel alloys, magnesium alloys.\n(Aluminium alloys are attacked by strong alkali and should not be used.)","answers":["Stainless steels, copper alloys, titanium alloys; nickel alloys commonly used","PE or PP lined steel pipe as a combination"]},{"id":"14.8","title":"Nickel plating on a steel component","q":"A steel component is nickel-plated. Would you expect the nickel to act as a passive or an active barrier to aqueous corrosion of the steel? Explain why.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Nickel\u0027s reduction potential (about −0.25 V) is higher (more noble) than iron\u0027s (about −0.44 V).\n2) In a corrosion cell formed at a scratch or hole, nickel becomes the cathode and steel the anode.\n3) So nickel is only a passive barrier: it protects while intact, but the steel corrodes (and is attacked preferentially) at holes or cracks. Zinc, by contrast, would be an active (sacrificial) coating.","answers":["Passive barrier only","Ni is more noble than Fe, so Ni is the cathode and steel corrodes at coating defects"]},{"id":"15.1","title":"Current and power in a thin nickel wire","q":"A potential difference of 0.05 V is applied across a nickel wire 100 mm long and 0.2 mm in diameter. The resistivity of nickel is 9.5 µΩ·cm. What current flows in the wire, and how much power is dissipated in it?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) ρ_e = 9.5 µΩ·cm = 9.5×10⁻⁸ Ω·m.\n2) A = π(0.1×10⁻³)² = 3.14×10⁻⁸ m². R = ρ_eL/A = 9.5×10⁻⁸ × 0.1 / 3.14×10⁻⁸ = 0.30 Ω.\n3) Ohm\u0027s law: i = V/R = 0.05/0.30 = 0.165 A.\n4) P = i²R = V·i = 0.05 × 0.165 ≈ 8.3×10⁻³ W.","answers":["R ≈ 0.30 Ω","i ≈ 0.165 A","P ≈ 8.3 mW"]},{"id":"15.2","title":"Capacitance with air and with a dielectric","q":"(a) A parallel-plate capacitor has plate area A = 0.1 m² separated by an air gap of t = 100 µm. What is its capacitance? (b) The air gap is replaced by a dielectric of thickness t = 1 µm with dielectric constant ε_r = 20 (same plate area). What is its capacitance now? Take ε_o = 8.85×10⁻¹² F/m.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) C = ε_r ε_o A/t, with ε_r = 1 for air.\n2) (a) C₁ = 8.85×10⁻¹² × 0.1 / 100×10⁻⁶ = 8.85×10⁻⁹ F = 8.85 nF.\n3) (b) C₂ = 20 × 8.85×10⁻¹² × 0.1 / 10⁻⁶ = 1.77×10⁻⁵ F = 17.7 µF.\n(Book prints (a) as \u00278.85 pF\u0027, a unit slip; 8.85×10⁻⁹ F is nF.)","answers":["(a) C ≈ 8.85×10⁻⁹ F (8.85 nF)","(b) C ≈ 1.77×10⁻⁵ F (17.7 µF)"]},{"id":"15.3","title":"Sizing a supercapacitor dielectric","q":"A supercapacitor must store an energy of E_c = 1 kJ when a potential difference V = 100 V is applied across its plates. The dielectric has ε_r = 10,000 and breakdown field E_b = 20 MV/m. What is the minimum dielectric thickness needed to avoid breakdown, and hence the minimum plate area required? Take ε_o = 8.85×10⁻¹² F/m.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Field V/t must stay below E_b: t ≥ V/E_b = 100/(2×10⁷) = 5×10⁻⁶ m = 5 µm.\n2) Stored energy E_c = ½CV², so C = 2×1000/100² = 0.2 F.\n3) C = ε_r ε_o A/t gives A = C t/(ε_r ε_o) = 0.2 × 5×10⁻⁶ / (10⁴ × 8.85×10⁻¹²) = 11.3 m².","answers":["t ≥ 5 µm","C = 0.2 F","A ≈ 11.3 m²"]},{"id":"15.4","title":"Heating of nylon by an oscillating field","q":"(a) An oscillating voltage of amplitude 100 V at frequency f = 60 Hz appears across a nylon insulator of thickness x = 1 mm. Nylon has a dielectric loss factor L = ε_r tan δ ≈ 0.1 and a volumetric specific heat ρC_p = 3×10⁶ J/m³·K. How much power is dissipated per unit volume in the nylon? Assuming no heat loss, how fast does the temperature rise? (b) If the frequency were instead f = 1 GHz, what would the heating rate be? Take ε_o = 8.85×10⁻¹² F/m.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Field amplitude E = V/x = 100/10⁻³ = 10⁵ V/m.\n2) Dielectric loss power per volume: P = f E² ε_o L (L = ε_r tan δ).\n3) (a) P = 60 × 10¹⁰ × 8.85×10⁻¹² × 0.1 = 0.53 W/m³. Heating rate dT/dt = P/ρC_p = 0.53/(3×10⁶) = 1.8×10⁻⁷ °C/s.\n4) (b) P ∝ f, so P = 0.53 × (10⁹/60) = 8.8×10⁶ W/m³; dT/dt = 8.8×10⁶/3×10⁶ ≈ 3 °C/s.","answers":["(a) P ≈ 0.53 W/m³; dT/dt ≈ 1.8×10⁻⁷ °C/s","(b) dT/dt ≈ 3 °C/s at 1 GHz"]},{"id":"15.5","title":"Electron mobility in pure copper","q":"The resistivity of pure copper is 2.32 µΩ·cm. Each copper atom provides one free electron. The atomic volume of copper is 1.18×10⁻²⁹ m³ and the electron charge is e = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C. What is the electron mobility in copper?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Free-electron density n_v = 1/(atomic volume) = 1/1.18×10⁻²⁹ = 8.47×10²⁸ m⁻³.\n2) ρ_e = 2.32×10⁻⁸ Ω·m, conductivity κ_e = 1/ρ_e = n_v e μ_e.\n3) μ_e = 1/(n_v e ρ_e) = 1/(8.47×10²⁸ × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × 2.32×10⁻⁸) = 1/314 ≈ 3.2×10⁻³ m²/V·s.","answers":["n_v ≈ 8.47×10²⁸ m⁻³","μ_e ≈ 3.2×10⁻³ m²/V·s"]},{"id":"15.6","title":"Intrinsic conductivity of silicon at room temperature","q":"The electron mobility in silicon at 300 K is μ_e = 0.14 m²/V·s and the hole mobility is μ_h = 0.04 m²/V·s. The carrier density is 1.5×10¹⁶ m⁻³ for both electrons and holes (intrinsic semiconductor). What is the intrinsic conductivity of silicon at 300 K? Take e = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Both carriers contribute: κ_e = n e μ_e + n e μ_h = n e (μ_e + μ_h) (n equal).\n2) κ_e = 1.5×10¹⁶ × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × (0.14 + 0.04) = 2.4×10⁻³ × 0.18 = 4.3×10⁻⁴ S/m.","answers":["κ_e ≈ 4.3×10⁻⁴ S/m"]},{"id":"15.7","title":"Polarisation of a displaced rock-salt ionic crystal","q":"An ionic crystal with the rock-salt (NaCl-type) structure is made of equal numbers of positive and negative ions, each carrying a charge of magnitude e = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C. The cubic lattice parameter is 0.45 nm, and the conventional cubic cell contains four ion pairs. An electric field displaces the positive ions relative to the negative ions by 1% of the lattice parameter. What is the resulting polarisation (dipole moment per unit volume)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1) Displacement Δx = 0.01 × 0.45 nm = 4.5×10⁻¹² m.\n2) Dipole per ion pair d = qΔx = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × 4.5×10⁻¹² = 7.2×10⁻³¹ C·m.\n3) Cell volume V = (0.45×10⁻⁹)³ = 9.1×10⁻²⁹ m³, containing 4 pairs.\n4) P = 4d/V = 4 × 7.2×10⁻³¹ / 9.1×10⁻²⁹ = 0.032 C/m².","answers":["d ≈ 7.2×10⁻³¹ C·m per ion pair","P ≈ 0.032 C/m²"]}];
